*** DEPRECATED >> related to this modifiers topic in Java 17

    1. strictfp - EFFECTIVELY REDUNDANT since Java 17 (JEP 306: "Restore Always-Strict Floating-Point
       Semantics") - not formally "deprecated" as a keyword removal, but its PURPOSE is gone
       >> before Java 17, strictfp forced strict IEEE 754 floating-point behavior (some platforms
          allowed slightly wider intermediate precision without it)
       >> Java 17 onwards - ALL floating-point operations are ALWAYS strictfp-like by default,
          the keyword is now a NO-OP retained purely for source compatibility
       >> can still be written (public strictfp class X {}) - compiles fine, does literally nothing
       >> safe to ignore/remove strictfp from new Java 17 code, no behavior change either way

    2. SecurityManager-based permission checks - DEPRECATED FOR REMOVAL (JEP 411, Java 17)
       >> relevant here because access-modifier enforcement (public/protected/etc.) is a LANGUAGE-level
          concept, separate from SecurityManager's runtime permission checks - don't confuse the two;
          SecurityManager is being phased out, but private/protected/public/default rules above are
          core language features and are NOT affected/deprecated at all